1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,159
Here are four sticks, joined at the corners by hinges.

2
00:00:04,159 --> 00:00:08,163
Push the top, and the square wobbles into a rhombus.

3
00:00:08,163 --> 00:00:12,168
Every stick keeps its length, yet the shape changes.

4
00:00:12,467 --> 00:00:18,035
Now try three sticks. However hard you push, the triangle keeps its shape.

5
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:23,744
A shape that can't wobble, except by sliding or turning as a whole, is called rigid.

6
00:00:23,744 --> 00:00:27,093
That's why bridges and cranes are full of triangles.

7
00:00:27,367 --> 00:00:32,453
Now go to three dimensions: a box of twelve sticks, with six faces.

8
00:00:32,453 --> 00:00:36,857
The rules: sticks keep their lengths, and faces stay flat.

9
00:00:36,857 --> 00:00:38,983
But a face may change shape.

10
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:46,260
The box still wobbles. It leans over, and each square face becomes a flat rhombus.

11
00:00:46,567 --> 00:00:52,631
Now meet the truncated dodecahedron: sixty corners, ninety sticks, twenty triangles,

12
00:00:52,631 --> 00:00:55,735
and twelve ten-sided faces called decagons.

13
00:00:56,067 --> 00:01:01,076
The triangles can't change shape. But each decagon is a ring of ten sticks that could

14
00:01:01,076 --> 00:01:04,023
flex within its plane. Can the whole solid wobble?

15
00:01:04,023 --> 00:01:05,379
Pause and make a guess.

16
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:11,697
In twenty twenty-six, three mathematicians tested thirty-one famous solids this way.

17
00:01:11,697 --> 00:01:14,767
This was the only one they couldn't decide.

18
00:01:14,767 --> 00:01:17,052
The answer is no. Let's see why.

19
00:01:17,967 --> 00:01:23,904
A first idea: count. In the flat square, each corner needs two numbers, so eight numbers.

20
00:01:23,904 --> 00:01:29,775
The sticks give four rules. And sliding or turning the whole square uses three freedoms.

21
00:01:30,067 --> 00:01:34,854
Eight minus four minus three leaves one: exactly the wobble we saw.

22
00:01:34,854 --> 00:01:38,998
For the triangle, six minus three minus three leaves zero.

23
00:01:38,998 --> 00:01:39,427
Rigid.

24
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:44,836
In space, each corner needs three numbers, and so does each face's flat plane.

25
00:01:44,836 --> 00:01:47,668
That's two hundred and seventy-six numbers.

26
00:01:47,967 --> 00:01:53,740
There are ninety stick rules, and a hundred and eighty rules saying a corner lies on

27
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:58,071
a face. Take away six ways to slide and turn, and zero is left.

28
00:01:58,367 --> 00:02:04,623
But the box also counts to zero, and the box wobbles: some rules quietly repeat each

29
00:02:04,623 --> 00:02:10,134
other. Euler's formula makes this count zero for every solid of this kind.

30
00:02:10,134 --> 00:02:12,071
Counting can never decide.

31
00:02:12,866 --> 00:02:16,057
To look closer, imagine a wobble just starting.

32
00:02:16,057 --> 00:02:21,285
At that first instant, each corner sets off in some direction, at some speed.

33
00:02:21,285 --> 00:02:22,778
Draw that as an arrow.

34
00:02:23,066 --> 00:02:28,097
For a stick not to stretch or squash at first, its two ends must move equally along the

35
00:02:28,097 --> 00:02:31,682
stick. So the shadows of their arrows on the stick must match.

36
00:02:31,966 --> 00:02:37,185
In the square, the top corners slide sideways and the bottom ones stay put.

37
00:02:37,185 --> 00:02:42,404
The upright sticks see two zero shadows; the top stick sees two equal ones.

38
00:02:42,404 --> 00:02:43,030
All pass.

39
00:02:43,300 --> 00:02:48,900
An arrow pattern that keeps every length unchanged at the first instant is called an

40
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:52,900
infinitesimal flex. Looking for one is the first-order test.

41
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:59,771
But passing that test isn't enough. Fix two sticks in a straight line, pinned at both

42
00:02:59,771 --> 00:03:02,700
ends, and push the middle joint sideways.

43
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,963
The arrow points straight across both sticks, so both shadows are zero.

44
00:03:07,963 --> 00:03:11,808
The first-order test is passed. Yet the joint is stuck.

45
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,834
Move the joint up by a small amount, t.

46
00:03:14,834 --> 00:03:20,583
By Pythagoras, each stick would need length the square root of one plus t squared.

47
00:03:20,583 --> 00:03:23,668
That's too long, by about half of t squared.

48
00:03:23,966 --> 00:03:29,263
That error grows like t squared, too slowly for the first-order test to see.

49
00:03:29,263 --> 00:03:32,469
Could nudging the joint along the line fix it?

50
00:03:32,469 --> 00:03:37,000
The left stick needs a nudge left, the right stick a nudge right.

51
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:37,766
Impossible.

52
00:03:38,066 --> 00:03:42,011
That clash is called a second-order obstruction.

53
00:03:42,011 --> 00:03:48,914
So the tests come in layers: errors like t, then t squared, then t cubed, and so on.

54
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:55,640
Now test our solid. The first-order test finds four independent infinitesimal flexes,

55
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:59,204
not counting slides and turns. Here is one of them.

56
00:03:59,533 --> 00:04:05,673
Any mix works too: v zero times the first, plus v one times the second, and so on.

57
00:04:05,673 --> 00:04:08,893
Four numbers describe every possible start.

58
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:14,507
Follow the arrows in a straight line, and the sticks begin to stretch, just as before,

59
00:04:14,507 --> 00:04:16,112
by amounts like t squared.

60
00:04:17,033 --> 00:04:22,051
Now the second-order test: bend the path to cancel those t squared errors.

61
00:04:22,051 --> 00:04:26,255
Here it always works: all forty second-order numbers are zero.

62
00:04:26,255 --> 00:04:29,849
That's where the twenty twenty-six paper had to stop.

63
00:04:30,133 --> 00:04:33,810
The bent path's errors now grow like t cubed.

64
00:04:33,810 --> 00:04:40,755
After cancelling all we can, four leftover errors remain, each a cubic in v zero to v

65
00:04:40,755 --> 00:04:41,245
three.

66
00:04:41,533 --> 00:04:46,875
A real wobble needs all four leftovers to be zero, for some choice of the four numbers

67
00:04:46,875 --> 00:04:51,037
that isn't all zeros. If only all zeros works, no wobble can start.

68
00:04:51,333 --> 00:04:57,765
Here's a small version. Take two numbers, x and y, and two leftovers: x squared minus

69
00:04:57,765 --> 00:05:04,197
y squared, and x times y. The first one alone is zero all along these diagonal lines.

70
00:05:04,466 --> 00:05:11,193
The second is zero along the axes. The only point on both is the origin, where x and

71
00:05:11,193 --> 00:05:12,074
y are zero.

72
00:05:12,366 --> 00:05:18,345
Algebra proves it. Multiply the first leftover by x, the second by y, and add.

73
00:05:18,345 --> 00:05:21,488
The middle terms cancel, leaving x cubed.

74
00:05:21,488 --> 00:05:25,014
So if both leftovers are zero, x must be zero.

75
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:31,389
For the solid, a computer found a certificate, a list of numbers anyone can check, that

76
00:05:31,389 --> 00:05:37,478
does the same. It combines the four cubics into v zero to the ninth power, and likewise

77
00:05:37,478 --> 00:05:39,508
for v one, v two and v three.

78
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:43,725
Finding those multipliers takes exact arithmetic.

79
00:05:43,725 --> 00:05:49,738
The solid's coordinates involve the golden ratio, about one point six one eight, whose

80
00:05:49,738 --> 00:05:51,556
square is itself plus one.

81
00:05:51,833 --> 00:05:55,654
Decimals bring rounding errors, and rounding can fake a wobble.

82
00:05:55,654 --> 00:06:00,324
A numerical search in the twenty twenty-six paper found what looked like one.

83
00:06:00,324 --> 00:06:02,993
With more careful numerics, it snapped back.

84
00:06:03,266 --> 00:06:07,936
So do the arithmetic on a clock with one thousand and nine hours.

85
00:06:07,936 --> 00:06:10,882
One thousand plus twenty lands on eleven.

86
00:06:10,882 --> 00:06:14,186
Every answer is exact, and numbers never grow.

87
00:06:14,466 --> 00:06:18,932
On this clock, six hundred and twenty-seven plays the golden ratio.

88
00:06:18,932 --> 00:06:24,864
Its square is three hundred and ninety-three thousand, one hundred and twenty-nine, which

89
00:06:24,864 --> 00:06:28,530
lands on six hundred and twenty-eight: itself plus one.

90
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:34,560
And because one thousand and nine is prime, division always works too.

91
00:06:35,333 --> 00:06:42,028
Here is the first leftover, in clock numbers: six hundred and forty-nine v zero cubed,

92
00:06:42,028 --> 00:06:44,597
plus nineteen more terms like it.

93
00:06:44,866 --> 00:06:50,008
Each square in this grid stands for one of the two hundred and twenty possible terms

94
00:06:50,008 --> 00:06:53,986
whose powers add up to nine. A lit square holds a nonzero number.

95
00:06:54,266 --> 00:06:59,445
Add the first leftover times its multiplier: two hundred and one squares light up.

96
00:06:59,445 --> 00:07:02,729
Add the second: one hundred and eighty-two stay lit.

97
00:07:02,729 --> 00:07:05,066
The third: one hundred and sixty-one.

98
00:07:05,333 --> 00:07:12,012
And the fourth. Everything cancels except one square: v zero to the ninth, with coefficient

99
00:07:12,012 --> 00:07:12,893
exactly one.

100
00:07:13,166 --> 00:07:16,614
The same happens for v one, v two and v three.

101
00:07:16,614 --> 00:07:22,908
So if all four leftovers vanish, so do all four numbers: no mix of flexes passes the

102
00:07:22,908 --> 00:07:24,182
third-order test.

103
00:07:24,466 --> 00:07:29,749
Terms beyond t cubed only add pieces whose powers add up to ten or more, and a standard

104
00:07:29,749 --> 00:07:33,634
result called Nakayama's lemma shows they can't rescue a wobble.

105
00:07:34,432 --> 00:07:38,689
But the real solid lives in the real numbers, not on a clock.

106
00:07:38,689 --> 00:07:41,200
Why should the clock's answer count?

107
00:07:41,499 --> 00:07:46,543
Start simple. If a whole number is zero, its clock reading is zero.

108
00:07:46,543 --> 00:07:50,307
So a nonzero reading proves the number isn't zero.

109
00:07:50,599 --> 00:07:56,163
A wobble is that kind of zero fact. If the real solid could wobble, its equations, read

110
00:07:56,163 --> 00:08:01,663
on the clock, would wobble too. The clock shows no wobble, so the real solid has none.

111
00:08:01,932 --> 00:08:06,922
The reverse fails: one thousand and nine reads zero on its own clock.

112
00:08:06,922 --> 00:08:11,117
A clock can invent a wobble, but it can't hide a real one.

113
00:08:11,117 --> 00:08:12,780
So one clock is enough.

114
00:08:13,566 --> 00:08:19,941
So what is proved? The truncated dodecahedron is locally rigid: any nearby shape with

115
00:08:19,941 --> 00:08:24,891
the same sticks and flat faces is the same solid, moved or turned.

116
00:08:24,891 --> 00:08:26,166
It cannot wobble.

117
00:08:26,499 --> 00:08:31,755
It says nothing about far-away shapes that no wobble could reach.

118
00:08:32,066 --> 00:08:37,310
The proof is computer-assisted. A small independent program, using exact arithmetic,

119
00:08:37,310 --> 00:08:42,616
rebuilds the solid and its equations from scratch and checks every identity, in under

120
00:08:42,616 --> 00:08:43,178
a second.

121
00:08:43,499 --> 00:08:47,027
Turning those identities into rigidity is a written proof.

122
00:08:47,027 --> 00:08:52,562
It was reviewed within the project, but not checked step by step by proof-checking software

123
00:08:52,562 --> 00:08:55,907
like Lean, and outside experts haven't refereed it yet.

124
00:08:56,832 --> 00:09:03,336
To recap. One: for solids like this, counting always balances, so it can't decide.

125
00:09:03,632 --> 00:09:09,744
Two: four infinitesimal flexes pass the first two layers of tests, but at the third layer,

126
00:09:09,744 --> 00:09:12,392
four cubic leftovers rule them all out.

127
00:09:12,666 --> 00:09:18,797
Three: on a clock with one thousand and nine hours, that algebra is exact, and a clock

128
00:09:18,797 --> 00:09:22,434
that sees no wobble proves the real solid has none.

129
00:09:22,732 --> 00:09:28,581
Still open: outside experts have yet to check the argument, far-away shapes are untouched,

130
00:09:28,581 --> 00:09:34,300
and this settles one solid, not a general rule for which solids need these deeper tests.
